---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between download and upload speed?',
    a: 'Download speed is how fast data reaches you, streaming, browsing, game downloads. Upload speed is how fast you send data, video calls, sending files, cloud backups. Most connections download faster than they upload; fibre is the exception, with near-symmetrical speeds.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a good ping?',
    a: 'Under 50 ms is good for most uses; under 30 ms is great for gaming. Above 100 ms, video calls feel delayed and games lag. Ping depends on the server’s location and your connection type, fibre is lowest, mobile data and satellite are higher.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is jitter?',
    a: 'Jitter is how much your ping varies from moment to moment. A steady 40 ms ping is fine; a ping that jumps between 20 and 150 ms causes stuttering calls and rubber-banding in games even though the average looks good. Under 30 ms of jitter is good.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Internet Speed Glossary', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/glossary' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Internet Speed Glossary: Ping, Jitter, FUP and More"
  description="Plain-English definitions of internet speed terms: bandwidth, throughput, latency, ping, jitter, packet loss, FUP, GPON, ONT, SNR, APN, Mbps vs MBps and more."
  canonical="/guides/glossary"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Glossary</p>
      <h1>Internet Speed Glossary</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Every term your provider, your router and this site use, in plain English,
        with what counts as "good" where it matters.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>Speed terms</h2>

      <h3>Bandwidth</h3>
      <p>The maximum capacity of your connection, the size of the pipe. Your package’s advertised speed (say 50 Mbps) is its bandwidth. What you actually get is throughput.</p>

      <h3>Throughput</h3>
      <p>The speed you actually measure, the water flowing through the pipe. A healthy connection delivers at least 80% of its bandwidth on a wired test at a quiet time.</p>

      <h3>Download speed</h3>
      <p>How fast data reaches you, streaming, browsing, game downloads. This is the number your package advertises.</p>

      <h3>Upload speed</h3>
      <p>How fast you send data, video calls, sending files, cloud backups. On DSL it’s typically just 1–1.5 Mbps; on fibre it’s close to the download speed.</p>

      <h3>Mbps</h3>
      <p>Megabits per second, how ISPs and speed tests measure speed. Not the same as MBps (megabytes per second): 1 byte = 8 bits, so 100 Mbps downloads at about 12.5 MB/s. See <a href="/guides/mbps-vs-mbps">Mbps vs MBps explained</a>.</p>

      <h3>Latency / Ping</h3>
      <p>How long a signal takes to reach a server and come back, in milliseconds (ms). Under 50 ms is good; under 30 ms is great for gaming. Fibre is lowest; mobile data and satellite are higher.</p>

      <h3>Jitter</h3>
      <p>How much your ping varies from moment to moment. A steady 40 ms ping is fine; a ping jumping between 20 and 150 ms causes stuttering calls and rubber-banding in games. Under 30 ms of jitter is good.</p>

      <h3>Packet loss</h3>
      <p>Data packets that never arrive, as a percentage. Anything above 1–2% causes noticeable problems, frozen video, lag spikes, dropped calls. Usually a sign of a bad line, weak Wi-Fi or congestion.</p>

      <h2>Connection terms</h2>

      <h3>DSL</h3>
      <p>Digital Subscriber Line, internet over copper telephone lines. Speed depends on your distance from the exchange and the condition of the line. PTCL DSL is the most widely available home internet in Pakistan.</p>

      <h3>FTTH / Fibre</h3>
      <p>Fibre to the home, internet over glass fibre cable, all the way to your house. Near-symmetrical uploads, low ping, and speeds that don’t degrade with distance. PTCL Flash Fiber, Nayatel, StormFiber and Transworld are all FTTH.</p>

      <h3>GPON</h3>
      <p>The technology most Pakistani fibre providers use to share one fibre cable between many homes. You don’t need to know how it works, just that it’s the standard for FTTH here.</p>

      <h3>ONT</h3>
      <p>Optical Network Terminal, the small white box that converts the fibre signal into an Ethernet connection for your router. Its LOS light turns red when the fibre signal is lost.</p>

      <h3>SNR (Signal-to-Noise Ratio)</h3>
      <p>A DSL line-quality figure, in decibels (dB). Above 10 dB is healthy; below 6 dB means the line is noisy and speeds will suffer. Found in your modem’s settings.</p>

      <h3>Attenuation</h3>
      <p>How much signal your DSL line loses over distance, in dB. Below 30 dB is good; above 50 dB means a long line and limited speed. Together with SNR, it explains most slow DSL lines.</p>

      <h3>FastPath / Interleaving</h3>
      <p>Two DSL line profiles. Interleaving corrects errors but adds latency (bad for gaming); FastPath skips the error correction for lower ping. PTCL DSL users can ask for FastPath on 1218, it only helps if the line is clean.</p>

      <h3>FUP (Fair Usage Policy)</h3>
      <p>A data cap on your package. After you use a set amount of data in a month, your speed drops until the next billing cycle. See <a href="/guides/ptcl-fup-data-cap">PTCL data cap (FUP) explained</a>.</p>

      <h3>DNS</h3>
      <p>Domain Name System, the internet’s phone book, translating website names into addresses. A slow DNS makes pages feel slow to start loading even on a fast connection. Switching to Cloudflare (1.1.1.1) or Google (8.8.8.8) DNS sometimes helps.</p>

      <h2>Mobile terms</h2>

      <h3>APN (Access Point Name)</h3>
      <p>The setting that tells your phone how to reach your mobile network’s data service. A wrong APN means slow or no internet. Each network has its own: zonginternet (Zong), internet (Telenor), ufone.pinternet (Ufone prepaid).</p>

      <h3>4G / LTE</h3>
      <p>The current standard for mobile data in Pakistan. National 4G averages run from 7.5 Mbps (Telenor) to 18.6 Mbps (Zong) on the PTA’s Q2 2026 benchmarking.</p>

      <h3>5G</h3>
      <p>The next generation of mobile data, launched in Pakistan on 19 March 2026. Median downloads around 220 Mbps where it’s live, but coverage is still pockets in 24 cities. See <a href="/guides/5g-in-pakistan">5G in Pakistan</a>.</p>

      <h3>Band n78</h3>
      <p>The 3.5 GHz radio band, Pakistan’s main 5G band, used by both Jazz and Zong. Your phone must support n78 to use 5G here.</p>

      <h3>Throttling</h3>
      <p>When a provider deliberately slows your connection, usually after a data cap (FUP) or at peak hours. Different from congestion, which is the network being busy rather than deliberately slowed.</p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Now test what the terms mean for you</h2>
        <p>Run a test and see your own download, upload, ping and jitter.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/understanding-your-results">Understanding your results →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
